<section class="section reveal" id="auth-security">
  <span class="section-label">Architecture Patterns</span>
  <h2>Authentication, Keys &amp; <span class="accent">Security</span></h2>

  <p>All Azure AI services require authentication. Azure provides multiple authentication methods, each suitable for different deployment contexts. Understanding these patterns is critical for building secure, scalable AI applications without exposing credentials.</p>

  <p>The simplest approach uses API keys — long-lived credentials granting full access to a service. For production systems, you should use Azure Managed Identity, which provides token-based access without storing long-lived secrets.</p>

  <div class="flow reveal">
    <div class="flow-step">
      <div class="flow-icon">1</div>
      <div class="flow-content">
        <strong>Development: API Keys</strong>
        <p>Quick setup for local development and prototyping. Store keys in environment variables, never in code. Rotate keys regularly every 90 days.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-step">
      <div class="flow-icon">2</div>
      <div class="flow-content">
        <strong>Production: Azure Managed Identity</strong>
        <p>Application automatically gets Azure tokens without handling secrets. Token is automatically rotated by Azure platform. Best practice for cloud-native apps.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-step">
      <div class="flow-icon">3</div>
      <div class="flow-content">
        <strong>Hybrid: Azure Key Vault</strong>
        <p>Centralized secrets management for API keys. Integrates with Managed Identity for token-based access to stored secrets.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Secure Configuration Pattern</h3>
  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="im">import</span> <span class="nm">os</span>
<span class="im">from</span> <span class="nm">azure.identity</span> <span class="im">import</span> <span class="bi">DefaultAzureCredential</span>
<span class="im">from</span> <span class="nm">azure.ai.textanalytics</span> <span class="im">import</span> <span class="bi">TextAnalyticsClient</span>

<span class="cm"># Production: Use DefaultAzureCredential (Managed Identity)</span>
<span class="va">credential</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">DefaultAzureCredential</span>()

<span class="cm"># Development fallback to API key from environment</span>
<span class="va">api_key</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">os</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">getenv</span>(<span class="st">"AZURE_AI_KEY"</span>)
<span class="va">endpoint</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">os</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">getenv</span>(<span class="st">"AZURE_AI_ENDPOINT"</span>)

<span class="va">client</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">TextAnalyticsClient</span>(<span class="va">endpoint</span>, <span class="va">credential</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We use DefaultAzureCredential, which automatically tries Managed Identity in Azure and falls back to environment variables locally. This single credential object works in both development and production without code changes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="compare-grid">
    <div class="compare-card">
      <h5>API Keys</h5>
      <ul>
        <li>Setup: Instant</li>
        <li>Rotation: Manual</li>
        <li>Audit: Basic</li>
        <li>Use: Development only</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card">
      <h5>Managed Identity</h5>
      <ul>
        <li>Setup: Minutes automatic</li>
        <li>Rotation: Automatic</li>
        <li>Audit: Full tracking</li>
        <li>Use: Production recommended</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card">
      <h5>Key Vault</h5>
      <ul>
        <li>Setup: Hours (complex)</li>
        <li>Rotation: Scheduled/on-demand</li>
        <li>Audit: Comprehensive</li>
        <li>Use: Compliance-heavy environments</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Use API keys for development, Managed Identity for production</li>
      <li>DefaultAzureCredential automatically handles credential selection</li>
      <li>Store all secrets in environment variables or Key Vault — never commit to code</li>
      <li>Rotate API keys regularly; Managed Identity handles rotation automatically</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

